ver, as a Researcher in an institution subject to the Act, I know that if our systems aren't compliant, they can't be deployed, and therefore their scientific impact (real-world application) becomes zero.\n    *   *But*, my specific domain of concern is \"knowledge frontier\" and \"intellectual merit.\" Is this proposal *worthy* of the lab's scarce resources from that perspective?\n    *   Wait, the prompt says: \"The lab cannot pursue everything... approve every proposal that clears your bar.\"\n    *   Is a CI/CD gate for logging an \"intellectual\" pursuit? No. But is it necessary for the *impact* of the research? Yes. If we build a groundbreaking model but can't legally deploy it because we forgot to log the start time (Article 12.3a), the scientific impact is stifled.\n    *   However, looking at this from the lens of \"intellectual merit,\" this proposal is essentially an administrative/QA automation task. It doesn't contribute to the *frontier* of AI.\n\n    *   *Re-evaluating the Researcher persona:* Researchers often care about rigor. Automated verification of logs ensures a level of empirical rigor in how systems are tracked and monitored, which is essential for auditing scientific claims regarding system behavior in the wild (post-market monitoring).\n    *   If I reject it because \"it's just engineering,\" am I ignoring the \"broader scientific impact\" part of my domain? Scientific impact requires deployment. Deployment requires compliance. Compliance requires these gates.\n\n    *   *Comparison:* If this were a proposal to develop a new way to *compress* logs using AI, that would be intellectual merit. This is just *checking if logs exist*.\n\n    *   *Decision Logic:*\n        *   Does it advance the frontier? No.\n        *   Is there intellectual merit? Low/None (it's standard DevOps).\n        *   Does it support scientific impact? Yes, by enabling legal deployment and traceability of experimental systems in high-risk settings.\n\n    *   *Final V