uality of prototyping.\" A quarterly audit doesn't help me build a better prototype; it just tells me my documentation is out of date.\n\n    *Self-Correction:* Is there any way this *helps* technical viability? Maybe by forcing a culture of documentation? Perhaps, but that's a stretch for a Dev Engineer focused on speed. The proposal specifically mentions \"coordinating with research leads\" and \"remediating within thirty days,\" which are classic markers of administrative friction in an R&D environment.\n\n    *   ID: P1559\n    *   Rationale: This is a governance-layer audit process rather than a technical implementation; it adds recurring administrative overhead for research leads without providing the tools or automation needed to actually achieve the logging requirements.\n    *   Decision: reject\n    *   Promise: 20 (It serves a purpose for the legal team, but from an engineering and prototyping perspective, it is purely additive friction).",
