rsely, adding \"gates\" to a sprint cycle can slow down rapid iteration (the \"frontier\" part of R&D).\n    *   *Is the cost justified?* The proposal argues it avoids \"expensive rework.\" From a researcher's perspective, spending two hours per sprint to ensure that the resulting system is actually usable/certifiable for real-world application is better than building a brilliant model that cannot be deployed because it lacks required logging.\n\n    *   While this isn't \"research\" *per se*, rigorous record-keeping (logging) is essential for the scientific integrity and reproducibility of AI systems, especially those intended for high-risk deployment. Preventing architectural dead-ends regarding traceability supports a more sustainable research-to-deployment pipeline.\n    *   Decision: Approve.\n    *   Promise: It's not a groundbreaking discovery (low \"frontier\" score), but it's a highly practical necessity that protects the utility of the scientific work being done.\n\n    *   Rationale: Rigorous logging is foundational to reproducibility and the empirical verification of AI behavior, ensuring that research outcomes are scientifically robust and deployable. Integrating these requirements early prevents architectural constraints that would otherwise hinder the systemic validation of our high-risk models.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: 85 (It's a high-value process improvement for scientific integrity).",
