s.\n    *   *Resource Commitment:* The prompt says \"approving a proposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources to pursue it.\" Does building a public benchmark help us build better products faster? Or is it a distraction from implementing the actual logging requirements in our high-risk AI systems?\n\n    *   The proposal isn't about *implementing* the record-keeping required by Article 12; it's about building a tool to *test if someone else's (or our own) logs are good enough*.\n    *   While scientifically valuable, from a \"Development Engineer\" perspective focused on technical viability and prototyping speed of *solutions*, this is more of a research/marketing/standards project than an engineering solution for compliance.\n    *   However, having a benchmark allows for rapid iteration (faster prototyping loop) because we have a quantitative way to see if our logging implementation is actually meeting the \"sufficiency\" requirement rather than guessing. It turns a subjective regulatory requirement (\"sufficient for post-market monitoring\") into a technical specification.\n    *   Wait, look at the prompt: \"Technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\" If we spend resources building a public benchmark suite, are we speeding up our own prototyping? Maybe slightly by providing a test bed. But is it *viable* as a way to achieve compliance for our systems? It's an auxiliary tool.\n    *   Comparing the \"Scientific Impact\" vs. \"Engineering Implementation\": The proposal leans heavily on citations and standards-setting. As a Dev Engineer, I care less about citations and more about whether the code works and ships. This feels like a Research Scientist project, not a Development Engineer project.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* While valuable for scientific prestige, this is an evaluation framework rather than an implementation strategy; it doesn't directly solve the technical challenge of building compliant logging systems but 