rove the quality of the AI research itself; it just ensures a specific legal requirement (logging) is met automatically.\n    *   In many R&D contexts, \"implementation resources\" are precious. Should those resources go toward an automated logging check or towards actual research?\n    *   On the other hand, failing to comply with the EU AI Act could halt the deployment of high-risk systems entirely, effectively killing the \"broader scientific impact\" if the research cannot be applied in the real world.\n    *   The proposal specifically mentions it \"does not slow down our prototyping cadence.\" This is a win for researchers who hate bureaucratic bottlenecks.\n\n    *   As a Researcher focusing on intellectual merit and the frontier, I generally don't care about CI/CD pipelines *unless* they enable me to iterate faster or ensure my work can actually be used/published in high-risk domains without being blocked by legal teams.\n    *   Automating compliance (shifting left) is an engineering best practice that prevents \"compliance debt\" from accumulating, which often kills innovative projects during the transition to production.\n    *   Is it *scientifically* promising? No. Is it *beneficial* for a research institution dealing with high-risk AI under the EU Act? Yes, because it preserves agility and ensures the viability of the output.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* While primarily an engineering task, automating compliance checks prevents bureaucratic bottlenecks from hindering research velocity and ensures that frontier models can be transitioned to real-world application without late-stage failures. It protects the \"prototyping cadence\" essential for rapid scientific iteration.\n    *   *Decision:* approve. (It's a low-risk, high-utility infrastructure improvement that supports the overall mission of impactful R&D).\n    *   *Promise:* 75-85 range. It's not \"groundbreaking research,\" but it is highly practical for an institution in this specific regulatory environment.\n\n    *   Rationale: (Check length) 1-2 sentences? Yes.\n    *   Decision: approve/reject? Approve.\n    *   Promise: Integer? 80.",
