ity (cryptographic chaining), eliminates discrepancies between logs and state, simplifies debugging/reproduction.\n    *   *Cons:* High complexity to implement as a *primary* architecture compared to simple logging; potentially high latency if not designed correctly; significant engineering effort to migrate an existing system to event sourcing.\n\n    *   As a Development Engineer concerned with viability and speed:\n        *   Is it viable? Yes.\n        *   Does it improve quality/speed? Long term, yes (reproducibility). Short term, it's a heavy lift.\n        *   Is the prototype plan reasonable? 3 months for one system is aggressive but doable if they use existing tools (like Kafka).\n    *   Comparing \"Structural Guarantee\" vs \"Procedural Enforcement\": This is a huge win for engineering quality. Instead of hoping engineers remember to call `log()`, the system cannot function without writing to the log.\n\n    *   Rationale: Moving from procedural logging to an event-sourced architecture provides structural guarantees for traceability and drastically simplifies reproduction of AI states, though it increases initial architectural complexity.\n    *   Decision: Approve. (The benefit to research quality and compliance certainty outweighs the implementation cost).\n    *   Promise: High, but not 100 because \"primary architecture\" is a disruptive change. Maybe 85-90.",
