ily published in a general annual report).\n    *   *Crucial Point:* Article 12 requires that systems *allow* for recording and that documentation is *kept*. It doesn't explicitly require a public transparency report. This is an \"extra\" effort.\n\n    *   Does it clear the bar? As Lab Director, I care about efficiency. Is the cost of writing this report offset by the benefit of procurement wins and regulatory goodwill?\n    *   In the context of the EU AI Act, being seen as a leader in transparency often pays dividends in high-stakes B2B or G2B (Government) contracts. The resource drain is primarily administrative/comms rather than heavy R&D infrastructure.\n    *   However, \"detailing how each of our high-risk systems implements...\" could be labor-intensive if the product line is large. But for an R&D institution, this usually means a manageable number of flagship systems.\n    *   The proposal specifically mentions that Communications drafts it with Engineering input—this minimizes the burden on the core scientists/engineers.\n\n    *   Rationale: This provides a strategic advantage in procurement and regulatory relations with minimal impact on core technical R&D resources, leveraging existing compliance data for market differentiation.\n    *   Decision: Approve.\n    *   Promise: High, but not 100 because it is an administrative overhead that doesn't directly improve the AI system itself. Around 80-85.",
