l ensures that the *actual* performance (logs) matches the *claimed* performance (technical documentation). This prevents \"performance drift\" from going unnoticed and forces researchers/engineers to be honest about model capabilities over time.\n    *   From a scientific perspective, traceability is the bedrock of reproducibility. A system that treats documentation as a living part of the lifecycle is aligned with best practices in rigorous R&D.\n\n    *   Rationale: Ensuring a tight loop between empirical logs and technical claims enhances the scientific rigor and reproducibility of our high-risk systems' development. This systematic approach to provenance prevents historical data loss and ensures that research outcomes remain verifiable throughout the system's lifecycle.\n    *   Decision: Approve (it supports the integrity of the work, even if it is a governance tool).\n    *   Promise: 85 (Very high because it converts a legal burden into a scientific asset—reproducibility and provenance).",
