        "reasoning_content": "Development Engineer at an R&D institution.\nTechnical viability, speed/quality of prototyping.\nEU AI Act Article 12 (Record-keeping) and Recital 71. High-risk AI systems must allow automatic recording of events (logs), ensure traceability for risk identification/post-market monitoring, and specific requirements for certain Annex III systems.\n\n        *   Rationale (1-2 sentences).\n        *   Decision (\"approve\" or \"reject\").\n        *   Promise score (0-100).\nProposal P2881: \"Internal Audit Trail and Retention Protocol.\" It's a governance framework involving named ownership, retention periods, quarterly audits, designated compliance engineers, a centralized health dashboard, and an escalation path.\n\n    *   The proposal focuses on *governance*, *compliance monitoring*, *ownership*, and *audit schedules*.\n    *   As a Development Engineer focused on *technical viability* and *speed/quality of prototyping*, I care about:\n        *   Does this hinder development? (Potentially, if it's too bureaucratic).\n        *   Is it technically feasible? (Yes, dashboards and logs are standard).\n        *   Does it solve the technical requirement of Article 12? Not directly. Article 12 requires that the *system* \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events.\" This proposal is about *managing* those records once they exist.\n        *   However, a centralized dashboard for log health actually helps engineers ensure their prototypes are meeting requirements before deployment, reducing late-stage rework.\n\n    *   The EU AI Act requires that systems \"technically allow\" logging. P2881 focuses on the *process* of ensuring those logs exist and are maintained.\n    *   From a dev perspective: This isn't a technical solution for *how* to log (e.g., using ELK stack, Prometheus, etc.), but rather how to ensure the logging is working across products.\n    *   While it's more \"compliance\" than \"engineering,\" the mention of a \"centralised dashboard showing real-time log health\" provides technical value by alerting developers to failures in their logging implementation immediately.\n    *   However, assigning \"one compliance engineer per product line\" might be seen as overhead that slows down prototyping if they become bottlenecks for every change. But generally, having clear ownership of log streams prevents the common engineering nightmare where no one knows who owns which log when things break.\n\n    *   Rationale: This proposal focuses on governance and monitoring rather than the technical implementation of logging, but a centralized health dashboard is highly valuable for identifying failures early in the development cycle. It ensures that the technical requirements are actually being met without requiring every developer to build their own audit framework from scratch.\n    *   Decision: Approve. (Even though it's governance-heavy, the \"real-time log health\" part is a win for engineering viability).\n    *   Promise: 75. It's solid and necessary, but doesn't solve the core *how* of logging—it just makes sure we don't forget to do it or break it.\n\n    *   Does it address Article 12? Yes, by ensuring the technical capabilities are functioning.\n    *   Is it viable? Yes.\n    *   Does it impact speed/quality? Quality increases; speed might take a slight hit due to audits, but is saved from catastrophic failure at the end.",
