nalysis:* Anonymizing logs from high-risk AI systems is notoriously difficult and resource-intensive (potential for membership inference or reconstruction attacks). The legal review process to clear these logs for public release would likely consume more resources than the \"data-science pod\" mentioned.\n    *   *Return on Investment:* While citations are good, the operational burden of a \"rolling monthly curation cycle\" is heavy. It turns a compliance requirement into a permanent product management task.\n\n    *   Rationale: Converting mandatory logs into an open dataset creates high long-term overhead for anonymization and legal vetting, diverting specialized data science resources away from core R&D toward dataset maintenance. The scientific gain of citations does not justify the continuous operational cost and potential liability of releasing high-risk system telemetry.\n    *   Decision: Reject.\n    *   Promise: Low (maybe 20-30). It's a nice academic idea, but as a Lab Director focused on *resource efficiency*, it looks like a \"forever project\" that adds risk without a direct boost to our primary AI development goals.",
