ns), making it easier to secure budget for high-quality logging infrastructure.\n    *   Risk: Data privacy (GDPR) and confidentiality of the \"input data\" mentioned in Art 12(3)(c). The proposal mentions \"tagging,\" but not anonymization/pseudonymization. As a Compliance Officer, I need to be wary of this, but as a screening exercise for *ideation*, the concept of using logs for research is sound and aligns with the goal of improving safety.\n\n    *   Rationale: This transforms a mandatory cost center into an R&D asset, potentially increasing the quality of our monitoring while justifying compliance expenditure through scientific output.\n    *   Decision: Approve. (It doesn't violate the AI Act; it leverages it).\n    *   Promise: High, but not 100 because GDPR/confidentiality hurdles will exist during implementation. Let's say 85.\n\n    *   Rationale: The proposal aligns with the spirit of post-market monitoring by actively analyzing logs to improve robustness and provides a strategic financial justification for the required compliance infrastructure.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: 85.",
