e can actually implement this in our systems. P2750 is heavily skewed toward research/theory (\"Bayesian degradation models\", \"new sub-field\"). While scientifically interesting, it doesn't provide the *mechanism* for record-keeping; it provides a way to analyze the records.\n    *   *Critique:* The prompt asks if this proposal should be pursued given scarce implementation resources. P2750 is an R&D project for \"generalisable methods,\" not a concrete engineering solution to implement logging in high-risk AI systems. It's too academic and detached from the immediate technical requirement of *implementing* logs.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* The proposal focuses on high-level statistical research and generalizable methodology rather than the actual implementation of the logging mechanisms required by Article 12. From a prototyping perspective, it is overly academic and risks becoming a theoretical exercise instead of a viable technical solution for record-keeping.\n    *   *Decision:* Reject. (It's not an engineering project to build logs; it's a research project to analyze them).\n    *   *Promise Score:* 30. It has some value for post-market monitoring, but as a \"proposal\" for responding to the record-keeping mandate, it misses the mark on technical implementation/prototyping speed.\n\n    *Wait*, let me re-read Article 12(2)(a): \"...logging capabilities shall enable the recording of events relevant for: (a) identifying situations that may result in... a risk\".\n    P2750 says they will develop methods to \"flag the situations presenting a risk.\"\n    To flag these situations, you *need* logs. But P2750 doesn't propose how to build the logs; it proposes how to analyze them using ML/Stats. For a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability and speed of prototyping, this feels like an \"over-engineered\" academic approach that ignores the fundamental plumbing (the actual logging system) in favor of fancy analysis tools.\n\n    *Comparison:*\n    Proposal