        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2948\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal confuses model-level interpretability with system-level event logging; requirements such as recording start/end times and user IDs are application-layer concerns that cannot be solved by neural architecture research. Investing 18 months into fundamental architectural changes to achieve what is essentially a software instrumentation task is technically inefficient and risks failing the regulatory requirement for operational logs.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 15\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Development Engineer at an R&D institution.\nTechnical viability, speed/quality of prototyping.\nEU AI Act Article 12 (Record-keeping), specifically focusing on automatic logging for high-risk AI systems to ensure traceability and risk identification.\n\n        *   Proposal ID: P2948\n        *   Title: Traceable-by-Design Neural Architecture Research.\n        *   Content: Researching model architectures where logging/provenance are intrinsic (not bolted-on). Four researchers, three candidate architectures, eighteen months. Measuring against Article 12(2) and standard benchmarks. Aim: combine regulatory logic with fundamental design.\n\n    *   *Technical Viability:* The proposal asks if architectural interpretability can match performance. This is a high-risk/high-reward research goal. While \"intrinsic\" logging sounds appealing, it's fundamentally different from traditional system logs (which record events like inputs, timestamps, and outputs). Article 12 focuses on *logging of events* over the lifetime of the system. Neural architecture design usually deals with how weights are updated or how information flows, not necessarily \"event logging\" in the operational sense (e.g., who used it, when it started/ended).\n    *   *Speed/Quality of Prototyping:* The timeline is 18 months for three architectures. In an R&D setting, this is a significant commitment. However, if we can make traceability intrinsic, we might reduce the overhead of external logging systems and improve reliability (less chance of logs being bypassed or corrupted).\n    *   *Alignment with Article 12:* Article 12 requires \"automatic recording of events (logs)\". Usually, this means software-level instrumentation. The proposal wants to move this into the *architecture*. While intellectually stimulating, it's a massive leap from standard logging. Does an intrinsic architecture actually fulfill the requirement for \"recording of events\" (like timestamps and user IDs)? Probably not on its own; you still need system logs.\n    *   *Resource Allocation:* Four researchers for 18 months is expensive. Is this a viable way to achieve Article 12 compliance, or is it an academic exercise? For a Development Engineer concerned with *viability* and *prototyping speed*, \"bolted-on instrumentation\" (the alternative) is exponentially faster, cheaper, and more reliable for meeting a regulatory deadline than inventing new neural architectures.\n\n    *   The proposal conflates *interpretability/provenance* within the model with *event logging* required by the law. Article 12(2) and 12(3) are about operational logs (start time, end time, database references). No amount of \"neural architecture\" will record the start date and time of a system's use—that's a wrapper/application layer function.\n    *   Spending 18 months researching architectures to solve a problem that is essentially a software engineering task (logging) is an inefficient use of R&D resources from a technical viability perspective.\n\n    *   Rationale: This proposal confuses model-level interpretability with system-level event logging; regulatory requirements for timestamps and user IDs cannot be solved through neural architecture alone and are more efficiently handled via standard instrumentation.\n    *   Decision: reject.\n    *   Promise: 15 (It's an interesting research topic, but a terrible way to implement Article 12).",
