perate, which is the ultimate \"speed\" killer (total shutdown).\n    *   The proposal targets the transition from prototype to deployment. This allows the prototyping phase to remain agile while ensuring production readiness.\n    *   It provides a structured way (checklist) to ensure technical requirements are met, reducing rework later.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* Placing the compliance gate at the transition from prototype to deployment protects prototyping agility while preventing costly late-stage redesigns or legal failures upon release. It ensures that logging capabilities—which can be architecturally complex—are treated as a first-class requirement for production readiness.\n    *   *Decision:* approve.\n    *   *Promise:* High (85). While manual gates have some friction, the risk of non-compliance with Article 12 is an existential threat to the product's deployment, making this a necessary technical safeguard.\n\n    *   Check: Did I act as Dev Engineer? Yes.\n    *   Check: Did I focus on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality? Yes.\n    *   Check: Did I follow the output format (rationale -> decision -> promise)? Yes.",
