loyments prevents \"compliance debt\" where we find out too late that our logging is broken in production.\n\n    *   Does it help the technical viability of the solution? Indirectly, by ensuring logs actually work.\n    *   Does it hinder speed/quality of prototyping? Yes, it's a significant amount of non-core engineering work (portal, auditing pipeline).\n    *   Is it \"overkill\"? The regulation requires logging capabilities. It does *not* require a client portal for aggregate compliance metrics.\n    *   From a Dev Eng perspective: I want to implement the logs in the system efficiently. I don't necessarily want to spend months building a secondary monitoring and reporting platform if we are trying to move fast on AI research/prototyping.\n\n    *Wait*, looking at it from another angle: If the lab is deploying high-risk systems, \"hope\" is not a strategy. An automated way to verify that logs are actually being generated across various environments prevents catastrophic failure during a regulator's audit. However, the *client portal* feels like an unnecessary addition to a technical requirement.\n\n    *Crucial point:* The prompt asks me to evaluate based on \"technical viability\" and \"speed/quality of prototyping\".\n    *   Building a custom audit tool and client portal is a diversion from core R&D.\n    *   The cost (engineering hours) outweighs the immediate technical benefit to the AI's performance or stability.\n    *   It transforms an engineering task (implement logs) into a product task (build a compliance dashboard).\n\n    *Rationale:* While verifying log integrity is useful, building a full-scale client portal and internal auditing framework consumes significant engineering resources that detract from core system prototyping without improving the underlying technical viability of the AI itself.\n    *Decision:* Reject.\n    *Promise:* Low to mid (maybe 30-40). It's a \"nice to have\" for legal/compliance, but an \"expensive distraction\" for Dev En