le 12 requires the system to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events.\" P2056 describes a research project (\"fundamental research thread,\" \"publish a taxonomy\"). It's not building the logging infrastructure; it's researching how to analyze the data once captured.\n\n    *   The proposal isn't about *implementing* the technical requirement (the logging mechanism itself), but about *optimizing the utility* of those logs for compliance goals (identifying risks).\n    *   However, as a Compliance Officer, my primary goal is ensuring we meet the legal mandate. The mandate is \"technical capability to log.\" This proposal spends resources on \"information-theoretic content\" and \"publishing taxonomies.\"\n    *   Wait—Article 12(2)(a) specifically requires logging capabilities that enable the recording of events relevant for identifying risks. If we just log everything randomly, we might not be meeting the *spirit* or *effectiveness* requirement of the law (traceability appropriate to purpose). Understanding \"drift semantics\" is directly applicable to fulfilling Article 12(2)(a) and (b).\n    *   Moreover, turning a regulatory burden into a research opportunity creates intellectual property and prestige without ignoring the mandate.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* This research directly supports our ability to meet Article 12's requirement for \"traceability\" and risk identification by defining what data actually signals a compliance failure. It transforms a mandatory cost center (logging) into a reputation-building asset.\n    *   *Decision:* Approve. (It helps the institution not just check a box, but actually be able to detect the risks they are legally required to monitor).\n    *   *Promise:* High, though it's more \"R&D\" than \"IT implementation.\" Let's say 85. It secures the \"how\" of compliance, which protects institutional survival.",
