g the gaps is wasteful.\n    *   Wait—the prompt asks if this should be *pursued*. Pursuing an audit helps define the technical requirements. Without it, the \"technical viability\" of the overall compliance effort is unknown.\n    *   The risk mentioned (institutional solvency/client loss) is a business concern, but the mechanism (mapping architecture to specific legal clauses) is a technical task.\n\n    *   Is it viable? Yes, auditing existing codebases and architectures is standard engineering practice.\n    *   Does it help prototyping quality? Yes, by providing clear specs for what needs to be added.\n    *   Is the timeline reasonable? Six weeks for an audit of \"every high-risk AI system\" depends on the portfolio size, but as a general directive, it's focused and time-bound.\n\n    *   Rationale: A technical gap analysis is essential to avoid haphazard implementation and ensures that engineering resources are targeted toward actual deficiencies in existing systems. It prevents costly late-stage refactoring by establishing a clear baseline of current logging capabilities.\n    *   Decision: Approve.\n    *   Promise: High (85-90). It's the logical first step for any technical implementation of a regulatory requirement.",
