ployment via CI checks ensures that compliance is \"baked in,\" reducing the risk of non-compliant systems reaching production.\n    *   *Efficiency (Institutional Survival/Budget):* The proposal explicitly aims to avoid the \"costly periodic audit scramble.\" This aligns with managing scarce resources effectively.\n    *   *Market Reputation:* Being able to demonstrate a rigorous, automated provenance and documentation pipeline is a strong signal of maturity and reliability to regulators and partners.\n    *   *Gap Analysis:* Article 12 focuses heavily on *logging events during operation*. P2086 focuses on the *technical documentation lifecycle* mentioned in Recital 71. While it doesn't implement the \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" for runtime operations, it implements the mandatory requirement to keep technical documentation up-to-date throughout the lifetime. It is a necessary pillar of the overall Article 12/Annex IV compliance strategy.\n\n    *   The proposal directly solves the \"up-to-date\" requirement of Recital 71 and Annex IV.\n    *   It mitigates the risk of regulatory sanctions by preventing non-compliant deployments.\n    *   It optimizes operational costs.\n\n    *   Rationale: The proposal directly addresses the Recital 71 mandate to keep technical documentation up-to-date throughout the system's lifetime, mitigating the legal and financial risks associated with audit failures through automated enforcement (CI checks). This transforms a high-risk manual burden into a sustainable engineering standard.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: 95 (Highly promising because it solves a critical compliance pain point using existing infrastructure like Git/CI, minimizing implementation friction while maximizing regulatory safety).",
